{
  "name": "What's Involved in a Curbless Shower Conversion?",
  "step": [
    {
      "name": "Step 1",
      "text": "Thinking about a curbless shower? It's a fantastic upgrade, but it's good to know what goes into it so you're prepared. It's much more than just taking out a curb; it's usually a full structural and waterproofing project. First off, we'll often need to open up your existing floor. This is to either recess the shower area or build up the surrounding floor to create that seamless, level entry. Then comes the crucial part: setting the drain. Depending on your layout, we'll choose the right drain type and make sure it's positioned to match the required slope—typically about 1/4 inch per foot towards the drain. Linear drains can actually simplify this by allowing a single-direction pitch, which is pretty neat. After the structural bits are handled, extensive waterproofing is key. This isn't just inside the shower but often extends beyond it to ensure that barrier-free entry remains leak-free. Finally, we'll handle the finish work, like tile, to bring your vision to life. It's a detailed process that really makes a difference in the long run. When you're ready to explore this for your home, give us a call to discuss the possibilities.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 1
    }
  ],
